<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1535 – after 1616</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Guaman Poma</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala was an Andean nobleman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> who wrote the Nueva corónica y buen gobierno, a chronicle of about 1,200 pages addressed to the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> around 1615. Illustrated with nearly 400 drawings, it describes Andean history and life and denounces the abuses of colonial rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, rediscovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1908, is a key source on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felipe-guaman-poma-de-ayala.jpg" alt="Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Guamán Poma de Ayala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felipe_Guam%C3%A1n_Poma_de_Ayala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
